/**
 * # Living phenomena
 *
 * Living individuals and biological material provide distinct descriptions of
 * living phenomena, without an intrinsic Earth restriction. Biology, ecology and
 * genetics identify Life as physical subject matter through about. An individual,
 * a material portion and a taxonomic classification retain their own meanings.
 */
ontology life
	version 1.0
	using imod, physical
	in domain imod:Life;

abstract identity TaxonomicIdentity
	requires authority TAXA {
        urn: "klab.authority.taxa",
        datasetKey: 312578
		codelists: {
        	species: "taxonomy.species",
        	genera:  "taxonomy.genera"
    	}
	};
    

/**
 * ## Living individuals
 *
 * A living individual is an Agent, recognizable as a unit capable of action on
 * itself or others. Agency here does not require deliberation, human likeness
 * or conscious intention. Its biological kind supplies criteria for unity and
 * continuity; observations and models establish particular individuals under
 * those criteria. A population or a shared classification does not by itself
 * establish one living individual.
 *
 * Individual remains an abstract organizing concept. Taxonomic identities
 * classify individuals without supplying their particular boundaries, proving
 * living presence or defining an exhaustive test for life.
 */
abstract agent Individual
    "An individual living organism, whose unity and continuity are established by the biological criteria applicable to its kind."
    ;

/**
 * ## Material of biological origin
 *
 * OfBiologicalOrigin characterizes the origin of an identified material body.
 * The material portion is a Subject; it is not thereby the same observational
 * entity as a living individual. Describing biological material does not assign
 * Agent nature, and describing a living individual does not retype it as a
 * material Subject. These descriptions establish no formal bodily relationship
 * between their referents.
 *
 * Biological origin does not establish current viability, death, cultivation,
 * harvest or intended use. Mixtures and transformed materials require their own
 * scope and provenance; a later product does not inherit every characterization
 * of its inputs automatically.
 *
 * A material portion is distinct from its measured mass. Biomass measurements
 * identify the biological material pool, its boundary and inclusion criteria,
 * together with units and a wet, dry or other stated measurement basis. Origin
 * alone does not define that pool or supply a mass value.
 */
attribute OfBiologicalOrigin
    "Having material origin in living organisms."
    applies to physical:MaterialBody;

/**
 * ## Cultivation and harvest
 *
 * Cultivated records deliberate establishment or management of a living
 * individual through cultivation. It characterizes the individual beyond its
 * momentary participation in an activity. A cultivated plant remains distinct
 * from a field, an assemblage and any material later obtained from it.
 *
 * Harvested refines biological origin for an identified material portion
 * obtained through harvesting. Harvested grain, fruit and timber share this
 * provenance without requiring their source organisms to have been cultivated.
 * Wild-picked fruit can be harvested; naturally fallen litter has biological
 * origin without thereby being harvested. Viable material is not excluded, and
 * harvesting alone does not establish biological death or retype an individual
 * Agent as a material Subject.
 *
 * Origin and intended destination can characterize why an entity is established
 * or maintained, beyond its current participation in a process. An intended
 * destination is distinct from actual use and a successful outcome: timber
 * intended for construction may later be used as fuel. Neither attribute alone
 * assigns a food, feed, fuel or economic role.
 *
 * Harvest history belongs to the identified portion. Changes in ownership or use
 * do not erase that history, and later products do not acquire it automatically.
 * The material is distinct from its measured mass; units and a wet, dry or other
 * stated basis belong to that measurement. Biological origin, crop identity,
 * product classification and taxonomic identity retain their separate meanings.
 */
attribute Cultivated
    "Having been deliberately established or managed through cultivation."
    applies to life:Individual;

attribute Harvested
    "Having been obtained as a portion of biological material through harvesting."
    is life:OfBiologicalOrigin
    applies to physical:MaterialBody;

/**
 * ## Grouped living entities
 *
 * A grouping can be articulated as an observable entity with its own qualities,
 * relationships and evolution. Its kind supplies the criteria by which it is
 * recognized as one group and continues through changes. A list selected by a
 * query does not alone establish that unity. Group descriptions and their
 * qualities remain distinct from descriptions of the individual members.
 *
 * Assemblage names the Subject reading: an identifiable grouped entity without
 * an attribution of agency to the group. CollectiveAgent names the Agent reading:
 * the worldview articulates the group as a unit capable of action on itself or
 * others. Neither kind specializes the other. Membership of individual Agents
 * does not by itself confer agency on their grouping, and a collective Agent
 * does not thereby become one organism.
 *
 * For example, a grouping of plants delimited for composition monitoring can
 * be articulated as an Assemblage. A vegetation association can be articulated
 * as a CollectiveAgent whose dynamics are described at group level. These are
 * explicit choices about the entity being described, rather than an automatic
 * inference from co-location or an absence of detailed observations. The same
 * grouped entity is not assigned both Agent and Subject nature.
 *
 * Collective agency does not require human-like intentions, planning or a model
 * of the internal interactions before the entity can be annotated. The worldview
 * states the unit-level meaning and scope; models describe its dynamics. Patterns
 * of qualities and relationships may help recognize or describe either grouped
 * entity without replacing it with a Configuration.
 *
 * Group quantities need not be simple aggregates of member quantities. Changes
 * in composition, relationships or extent are interpreted under the group's
 * criteria for unity and continuity. Membership change alone does not determine
 * whether the group persists or a different group has been established.
 */
abstract thing Assemblage
    "An identifiable grouping of living individuals, with stated criteria for its unity and continuity."
    ;

abstract agent CollectiveAgent
    "An identifiable grouping of living individuals capable of acting as a unit."
    ;

structural directed relationship SocialConnection;

abstract agent SocialGroup 
	emerges from life:SocialConnection;